  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nThe large upper low that extends from the Ohio Valley to the \nnortheastern seaboard continues to move slowly to the north and \neast, while another upper system in the Pacific northwest and into \nSouthern Canada is on an east southeast track.  The Plains remain \ngenerally in between, and milder conditions are expected today and \ninto Saturday. A quick glancing blow from a shortwave embedded in \nthe flow may bring increased cloud cover on Friday afternoon, but \nthe rain chances hold off for Saturday evening into Sunday morning.  \n\nOn Saturday, the models have trended toward a faster onset of rain \nto the area, with the north central Kansas counties potentially \nseeing showers start by Saturday afternoon, spreading into eastern \nKansas by Saturday evening. Some of the guidance has amounts in the \nquarter to half inch range for total precipitation. This seems\nplausible given the upper divergence, track of the sfc/850mb low and\nmid level frontogenetic forcing as it passes. Worth noting that \nsoundings are saturated and below freezing just above the surface on \nthe back side of the low, so would not be surprised to see a brief \nrain snow mix as the precip ends later Saturday night with snow \nmelting as it falls or as it comes in contact with warmer surface \ntemperatures (wet bulbs are still forecast at or above freezing). \nAll in all Saturday night looks miserable with cold, windy and rainy \nconditions. The system moves east quickly, and may get back into the \nsunshine before Sunday afternoon is over.\n\nZonal flow over the central US brings a return of the surface lee \ntrof, followed by some amplification as a western trof moves into \nthe four corners on Wednesday, increasing warm advection and \nbringing highs into the 60s for Wednesday. Rain chances return \nThursday into Friday as the low lifts across the plains toward the \nGreat Lakes, but still some run to run model differences on timing \nand strength",
